# Next.js App Router Conventions

## Server vs Client Components

- **Default to Server Components.** Only add `"use client"` when the file actually needs:
  - React hooks (`useState`, `useEffect`, `useRef`, …)
  - Browser APIs (`window`, `localStorage`, `IntersectionObserver`, …)
  - Event handlers attached to DOM elements
  - `motion/react` (which is client-only)

- Never mark `layout.tsx` or `page.tsx` as a Client Component unless unavoidable. Push the boundary into a leaf component instead.

```tsx
// ✅ Server-rendered page composes a client island
// app/(root)/page.tsx
import HeroSection from "@/components/sections/hero-section"; // "use client" lives there

export default function Home() {
  return <HeroSection />;
}
```

## Metadata

Define `metadata` and `viewport` exports per route. Use the `Metadata` and `Viewport` types. The root metadata is in `src/app/layout.tsx`; per-route titles are templated via the `template` field.

## Images

`next/image` with `images.unoptimized: true` is currently set in `next.config.js`. When adding remote sources, extend the existing `remotePatterns` list — don't disable security checks.

## Fonts

Loaded via `next/font/google` in `layout.tsx`. To add a font, declare it there and expose it via a CSS variable (e.g. `--font-foo`) so it can be referenced from Tailwind.

## React 19 specifics

- `forwardRef` is **no longer needed** for new components — accept `ref` as a regular prop.
- `useFormStatus`, `useActionState`, and the `use()` hook are available; prefer them for form state when the form is server-action driven.
- Keep existing shadcn components on `forwardRef` (they're vendored and should mirror the registry).
